artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a fully decentralized learning setting in which a finite set of client nodes are connected via a (possibly time-varying) communication graph, with no central coordinator or parameter server. Each client i holds a local data distribution D_i and a local loss f_i(w) = E_{z~D_i}[ℓ(w; z)], where ℓ is the per-example loss of a (non-convex) deep model parameterized by w ∈ R^d. The global objective is

    F(w) = (1/n) Σ_{i=1}^n f_i(w).

Clients perform stochastic-gradient-based updates and exchange messages only with their current graph neighbors. The protocol must operate under the following adversarial conditions:
 (a) clients may be temporarily unavailable, drop out permanently, or join during execution;
 (b) messages between neighbors may be lost, delayed, or arrive out of order;
 (c) the local distributions {D_i} are non-IID across clients;
 (d) clients perform local updates at heterogeneous and possibly time-varying frequencies;
 (e) the underlying communication topology may be chosen or may change over time.

Design a fully decentralized stochastic-gradient algorithm for training deep (non-convex) models in this setting and prove that it converges to a stationary point of F under conditions (a)–(e), with provable robustness guarantees and competitive practical (wall-clock) convergence time. In particular, characterize the trade-offs between dropout/joining rates, message-loss probabilities, degree of data heterogeneity, update-frequency disparity, and the chosen communication topology, and determine to what extent these can be simultaneously tolerated without degrading convergence.

